<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	Universal Links (backend/app/routers/linking.py's /i/{code} and /t
	pages). Without this entitlement, iOS never even asks
	https://<domain>/.well-known/apple-app-site-association for
	verification — it just always opens the URL in Safari — no matter
	what Settings.ios_app_id is set to on the backend. This file is the
	other half of that story that a backend env var alone can't cover.

	"applinks:yourdomain.com" is a placeholder: replace yourdomain.com
	with the exact host from backend's Settings.PUBLIC_WEB_BASE_URL
	(no scheme, no path, e.g. "teamira.app" for
	"https://teamira.app") before shipping a build that should support
	Universal Links. Until then this entitlement quietly does nothing
	useful (there's no real domain to verify against), which is the
	same "unconfigured, not broken" state as an empty
	Settings.ios_app_id on the backend side — every link still opens
	via the seenly:// custom scheme (already declared in Info.plist) or
	the /i/{code} / /t landing page's own JS redirect instead.

	Also needs, once a real domain is chosen:
	  - Settings.ios_app_id back on the backend, as "TEAMID.bundle.id"
	    (Apple Team ID + com.example.seenly, or whatever the bundle id
	    is by then).
	  - This file's CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS wiring in
	    Runner.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj (already done) picked up by an
	    actual signed build — associated domains are verified by the
	    OS against Apple's servers, not locally, so this can't be
	    tested in the simulator; a real device build is required.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:yourdomain.com</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
